From e346cbfd9448f41f8deaa3c234e521c521ae29dd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 13:02:03 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Widgets: draw rectangles a widget declares inside it at
 their own depth

A widget that paints several surfaces in one widget, such as a document
viewer's desk and the pages on it, can now say where each lies: the dynamic
property _q_stereo_depth_rects, a list of maps with a "rect" (in the
widget's coordinates) and a "depth" (a fraction of one step of depth inside
a window, from -1 behind what the rectangle lies on to 1 in front of it).
A program never gives pixels: the step is the person's element limit,
which the platform hears from the compositor (_q_stereo_element_limit,
pixels of a view 1920 wide), taken on the window's screen as that share of
the screen's width, in device pixels, never by the size of the window or
of an element.

Declaring makes the window follow its depth with the compositor
(_q_stereo_follow_depth through the native interface), so it is painted
once per eye while the compositor says it is awake (_q_stereo_awake) and
one step is at least half a device pixel, whatever style draws it; a style
that shows depth (_q_stereo_depth) still does as before.

While a window is painted per eye, such a widget is drawn a rectangle at a
time, each moved by its own eye shift in whole pixels by the rule the
repaint manager moves widgets by: nearer than what it lies on, drawn whole;
deeper, seen through its own rectangle, cut to it. The strip of what lies
beneath that a nearer rectangle's shift uncovers, which the widget never
drew, continues what lies beneath from the column just outside the
rectangle, so the rectangle's edge keeps its own depth in both eyes. Child
widgets lie on the widget's own plane.

Without the property, without the compositor's word, with the element
limit at 0 or on a window lying behind the active one, the window is
painted once, as before (tst_qwidgetrepaintmanager paintRectsAtTheirDepth).

